From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 11:35:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Abort a global's unfinished transactions when it
 goes away

When a window or worker went away, its IndexedDB connections were
closed, but its transactions were left alone. A readwrite transaction
it left running kept its SQL transaction open on the database's only
connection: every later writer of the database waited for it, and its
writes were neither committed nor rolled back. Closing a connection
with the forced flag aborts its transactions ("close a database
connection", step 2), so the factory now aborts its unfinished
transactions in the backend before it closes its connections, and
reports them finished.

For that, an abort no longer waits for a running batch of the
transaction to finish: the engine already runs the rollback after the
batch, and before any batch the scheduler hands over later. Waiting
let a TransactionFinished that arrived first hand the next writer's
batch to the engine ahead of the rollback. A transaction that finishes
without a commit or an abort is now rolled back too, so that none
leaves its SQL transaction open.

An upgrade requested while a removed iframe still had its connection
open waited for that connection's versionchange event, which the gone
global never fires. A connection that closes now no longer owes its
versionchange event; when its closing leaves no event to wait for
while other connections are still open, the request gets its blocked
event.

Tests: an abort that arrives while a batch of the transaction runs,
followed at once by the transaction's end, leaves nothing behind and
lets the next writer run; a transaction that ends without a commit or
an abort is rolled back; an upgrade waits for no versionchange event of
a connection that closed, and gets its blocked event when the others
stay open. A fork WPT test,
tests/wpt/mozilla/tests/IndexedDB/removed-iframe-aborts-its-transactions.html,
removes an iframe in the middle of a readwrite transaction and checks
that its writes are gone, that writers of the same and of other stores
run, and that an upgrade requested right away succeeds.

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits` at this commit: 105 passed, 1 skipped (before: 102). The abort and finish tests fail 3 of 3 with the abort deferral restored or without the rollback on TransactionFinished, and the closed-connection test times out without the change to close_database. /_mozilla/IndexedDB/removed-iframe-aborts-its-transactions.html: the first subtest timed out before this change, the second still did without the close_database change; both pass 10 of 10 on host SpiderMonkey and QuickJS, and on the DAYU200. DAYU200 at the tip of the series, `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage -- [--no-default-features] --features ohos-rdb-backend --status-level all`: RDB only 106 passed, 3 skipped; both backends 182 passed, 4 skipped (the skips are the documented test_new_storage_threads_create_independent_groups and the ignored long tests). WPT IndexedDB/ at the tip of the series (with the P1 review fixes), passing subtests of 4,511: host SpiderMonkey 3,251 with no unexpected result, host QuickJS 3,253, DAYU200 in the board configuration (QuickJS + RDB) 3,247 (before the series 3,224, 3,229 and 3,222); no new failures: QuickJS idbversionchangeevent.any.sharedworker timed out once and passed 20 of 20 on both host engines, and the board's idbversionchangeevent.any.worker saw a blocked event from the sharedworker variant, which uses the same database name and whose connection deleteDatabase doesn't close yet; the other differences are known flips. idbtransaction-oncomplete, window and worker, `--repeat 30 --processes 4`, at the tip: 60 of 60.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent wrote the change and its tests from the P1 review's finding and reproducer, and ran them on the host and the DAYU200)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
